## Module B – Smart Matching Algorithm

Core backend functionality

This is the most important technical component of your work.

You must develop an algorithm that compares Lost and Found reports and identifies possible matches.

### B1. Compare item names

Example:

- Lost: Black wallet.
- Found: Wallet.

The system should recognize that both reports may refer to the same type of item.

You should handle differences in uppercase/lowercase, extra spaces and common wording.

### B2. Compare item categories

Example:

- Lost category: Accessories.
- Found category: Accessories.

Matching categories should increase the score.

### B3. Compare colours

Example:

- Lost colour: Dark blue.
- Found colour: Navy blue.

You can initially use normalized colour comparisons and carefully defined equivalent terms.

### B4. Compare descriptions

Example:

Lost description:

"Black leather wallet with a small silver button."

Found description:

"Leather wallet, black colour, silver button."

Your algorithm should identify similarities between these descriptions.

Start with text normalization and keyword-based comparison. More advanced NLP can be added if time permits.

### B5. Compare locations

Example:

- Lost location: College library.
- Found location: Near college library.

You should identify whether the locations are the same or reasonably close.

Do not treat two unrelated locations as a match simply because their text looks similar.

### B6. Compare dates and times

Example:

- Lost date: October 30.
- Found date: October 31.

A nearby date should generally contribute more to the matching score than a very distant date.

### B7. Calculate matching scores

You can begin with this proposed scoring model:

| Matching criterion | Weight |
| ------------------ | ------ |
| Item name          | 20%    |
| Category           | 15%    |
| Colour             | 15%    |
| Description        | 20%    |
| Location           | 20%    |
| Date               | 10%    |
| Total              | 100%   |

The score is calculated using the similarity of each criterion.

For example:

